KG42.register("nodes-db5d8551467a8cd9d909b5f4",{"id":"COMP-486b93e7e3eb4336","code":"OMC002","label":"Permitted counterparty scope for negotiation and trading","definition":"The scope within which entities can negotiate or trade is limited through communication, partner sets, or contractual relationships; obtaining eligibility does not equal actual pairing or transaction conclusion.","layer":"L4","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"matching.counterparties","concept_ids":["matching.counterparties"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Permission: negotiation or trading permission limited between pairs of entities","Input: communication relationships, partner sets, or contractual arrangements","Output: the range of potential counterparty relationships; it does not directly specify the transaction pair"]},"exclusions":[["The study adopts a full peer-to-peer market with a complete communication graph, in which each participating entity can in principle negotiate with any other entity.","Neighbors represent market communication connections, not physical adjacency.","The forward-market communication structure is distinguished from the actual power grid structure; actual network constraints are considered in the balancing stage."],["When m∉ω_n, p_nm=0; eligibility within the set does not force a transaction.","When power bounds permit, an entity may exit with all bilateral transaction quantities equal to zero.","Electrical distance charges encourage trading with neighboring partners; high zonal rates discourage transactions into that zone.","During a contingency, new unit rates may induce a shift to partners unaffected by the fault while retaining the original negotiation process.","Dynamically modifying the partner set during congestion is only a possible scheme discussed."],["The basic structure permits DSO-aggregator negotiations and negotiations between an aggregator and the specific prosumers to which it can connect.","When an aggregator is not needed, direct prosumer-DSO trading can be added.","Closer-to-real-time trading among prosumers can be used to fulfill existing obligations, but this extension is not yet part of the day-ahead arrangement in this paper."]],"members":[{"id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R005::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"Any agent is in principle permitted to negotiate with any other agent. Scope: Full peer-to-peer market with the complete communication graph assumed in the study.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Full peer-to-peer market with the complete communication graph assumed in the study.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"a4ec968d194c84c79d11b8c5f4b1ec32c385825f73e90d4fb52db5cab724b54d","paper":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","rule_ref":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R005","node_id":"matching.counterparties","function":"matching.counterparties","unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::matching.counterparties","mechanism_id":"M01","anchor":true},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The trading partnership set restricts demand proposals: the bilateral quantity must be zero for a counterparty outside that set. Scope: Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: When a prospective counterparty is outside the requesting agent's trading partnership set. Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration. Participants update requested quantities through their own optimization (6a) and individually send the resulting trade proposals to their trading partners; the negotiation process repeats until convergence. Scope: Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: During each iteration of the negotiation process, until convergence. Requested quantities must be updated consistently with the agent's total-power bounds, with total power equal to the sum of its bilateral proposals. Scope: Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. Condition: At every negotiation iteration. Each prosumer's requested bilateral quantity must remain within that prosumer's power boundaries. Scope: Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). Condition: When the requesting agent is a prosumer. The prescribed update prohibits buying requests from generators by constraining their bilateral proposed quantities to be nonnegative. Scope: Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). Condition: When the updating agent is a generator.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). | Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). | Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. | Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). | Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. | Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.demand","source_rule_sha256":"dd9010023e8d5e8a6fc38f03180aa098dab283f108a8e7f7d06a7719cdb0d88c","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001","node_id":"matching.counterparties","function":"matching.counterparties","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.supply","source_rule_sha256":"eb3e04d94a468918abeb09de0a73b5857fe82afe93cb43afba9b3825477aa259","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002","node_id":"matching.counterparties","function":"matching.counterparties","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents. A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price. Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners. The bilateral trade power from agent n to agent m must be zero when m is outside n’s partnership set. Scope: Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n’s trading partnership set. Partnership sets specify possible trades and do not themselves enforce market participation. Scope: Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation. Partnership sets could be dynamically adapted in response to congestion; this is a proposed possibility rather than an asserted implemented procedure. Scope: Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. Condition: Congestion occurs.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. | Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. | Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. | Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. | Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. | Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C006"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"17c2f49b0636db1a4fd2c1e6da9d2ec09881219b8198137ad05850f17c990419","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005","node_id":"matching.counterparties","function":"matching.counterparties","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements may limit which participants can negotiate flexibility transactions with one another. Scope: Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market. The networked arrangement permits the DSO to negotiate with intermediary aggregators and each aggregator to negotiate with certain prosumers. Scope: Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can be incorporated when aggregators are not required. Scope: Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. Condition: Aggregators are not required. The possible extension could include prosumer-to-prosumer transactions, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil previously negotiated flexibility obligations. Scope: Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. Condition: Additional trading closer to real-time is added.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market. | Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. | Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. | Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E06","v141-b-g01-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"15a22ff1163b4cb25653da0c2dad7bf4c2e3745c5ee2ecf614a93cacf865532b","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004","node_id":"matching.counterparties","function":"matching.counterparties","unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["13-consensus_product_differentiation","17-exogenous_cost_allocation","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-matching","page":2,"printed_page":"995","section":"II-A. Peer-to-Peer Trading","anchor_text":"s, nd ng Fig. 1. Pool-based (a) and peer-to-peer (b) market structures. s- s- trading scheme. As suc","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For simplicity, only full peer-to-peer markets are considered here, i.e., with a complete graph as in Fig. 1(b). This graph is for the communication among agents since, in a peer-to-peer market, it is assumed that in principle any agent should be able to negotiate with any other agent. These are market constructs only, regardless of the actual power network (being, e.g., transmission and distribution grids, or possibly a microgrid), similarly to pool-based structures for forward markets, for which actual network constraints and considerations are considered at the balancing stage only. Therefore, when using the term \"neighbor\" in the following, this does not necessarily relate to physical location, but to a direct connection on the market communication graph.","paper":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"For simplicity, only full peer-to-peer markets are considered here, i.e., with a complete graph as in Fig. 1(b). This graph is for the communication among agents since, in a peer-to-peer market, it is assumed that in principle any agent should be able to negotiate with any other agent. These are market constructs only, regardless of the actual power network (being, e.g., transmission and distribution grids, or possibly a microgrid), similarly to pool-based structures for forward markets, for which actual network constraints and considerations are considered at the balancing stage only. Therefore, when using the term \"neighbor\" in the following, this does not necessarily relate to physical location, but to a direct connection on the market communication graph."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma_{nm}^{\\mathrm{dist}}=\\pm\\frac{u^{\\mathrm{dist}}d_{nm}}{2}, \\forall(n,m)\\in\\Omega\\times\\omega_n, (11); where d_nm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{\\mathrm{dist}} is expressed in €/MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma_{nm}^{\\mathrm{dist}}=\\pm\\frac{u^{\\mathrm{dist}}d_{nm}}{2}, \\forall(n,m)\\in\\Omega\\times\\omega_n, (11); where d_nm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{\\mathrm{dist}} is expressed in €/MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma^{dist}_{nm} = \\pm \\frac{u^{dist} d_{nm}}{2}, \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in \\Omega \\times \\omega_n, (11) where dnm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{dist} is expressed in € /MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma^{dist}_{nm} = \\pm \\frac{u^{dist} d_{nm}}{2}, \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in \\Omega \\times \\omega_n, (11) where dnm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{dist} is expressed in € /MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"opa DC power ﬂow approximation of the entire network, is bet- onter suited for meshed networks such ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"While the unique cost allocation policy does not differentiate agents, the electrical distance one might individualize too much grid tariffs. At the image of Danish public transportation system, using a zonal cost allocation policy seems a good compromise between both. In this situation the electrical network would be divided in several zones associated to distinct zonal unit fees. Each zone could be managed by a different system operator. A zone with a high price would incite outside agents not to trade with agents within, and push within agents towards self-consumption. In this sense, the zonal cost allocation policy allows to economically isolate an area. However, its efficiency strongly depends on zones’ design.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"While the unique cost allocation policy does not differentiate agents, the electrical distance one might individualize too much grid tariffs. At the image of Danish public transportation system, using a zonal cost allocation policy seems a good compromise between both. In this situation the electrical network would be divided in several zones associated to distinct zonal unit fees. Each zone could be managed by a different system operator. A zone with a high price would incite outside agents not to trade with agents within, and push within agents towards self-consumption. In this sense, the zonal cost allocation policy allows to economically isolate an area. However, its efficiency strongly depends on zones’ design."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"Note that the deﬁnition of partnership set ωn only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not ","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Note that the definition of partnership set \\omega_n only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not enforce participation. In other words, agent n keeps the possibility to opt-out of the market, i.e. with outcome (p_{nm})_m=0, if its power boundaries allow it.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"Note that the definition of partnership set \\omega_n only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not enforce participation. In other words, agent n keeps the possibility to opt-out of the market, i.e. with outcome (p_{nm})_m=0, if its power boundaries allow it."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"to obtain ﬂexibility [42]. ng In [43], it is recognized that within existing electricity mar- o- ket","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"This paper introduces a decentralized market design, which allows a DSO to manage local demand constraints by obtaining flexibility from competing aggregators, which must in-turn incentivize prosumers to provide this flexibility. The novel networked market structure accounts for DSO-to-aggregator and aggregator-to-prosumer ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements, which may limit which participants can negotiate transactions with one another.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"This paper introduces a decentralized market design, which allows a DSO to manage local demand constraints by obtaining flexibility from competing aggregators, which must in-turn incentivize prosumers to provide this flexibility. The novel networked market structure accounts for DSO-to-aggregator and aggregator-to-prosumer ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements, which may limit which participants can negotiate transactions with one another."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"p gg g q g the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in m the market are generical","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"It is assumed that the DSO is responsible for ensuring the total demand in the distribution network does not exceed a maximum limit. The proposed flexibility market design allows the DSO to incentivize demand flexibility from local prosumers to achieve this. ICT infrastructure and contractual agreements may be needed to facilitate the procurement of flexibility. Therefore, rather than requiring the DSO to negotiate directly with each prosumer, the proposed flexibility market is designed based on a networked structure, with the DSO able to negotiate with intermediary aggregators, and each aggregator in-turn able to negotiate with certain prosumers. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can also be incorporated if aggregators are not required. Throughout the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in the market are generically referred to as agents.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"It is assumed that the DSO is responsible for ensuring the total demand in the distribution network does not exceed a maximum limit. The proposed flexibility market design allows the DSO to incentivize demand flexibility from local prosumers to achieve this. ICT infrastructure and contractual agreements may be needed to facilitate the procurement of flexibility. Therefore, rather than requiring the DSO to negotiate directly with each prosumer, the proposed flexibility market is designed based on a networked structure, with the DSO able to negotiate with intermediary aggregators, and each aggregator in-turn able to negotiate with certain prosumers. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can also be incorporated if aggregators are not required. Throughout the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in the market are generically referred to as agents."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Only day-ahead trading is considered, but additional trading closer to real-time could be added, allowing agents to renegotiate based on updated demand predictions. In this case, prosumer-to-prosumer transactions could be included, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil their previously negotiated flexibility obligations.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Only day-ahead trading is considered, but additional trading closer to real-time could be added, allowing agents to renegotiate based on updated demand predictions. In this case, prosumer-to-prosumer transactions could be included, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil their previously negotiated flexibility obligations."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["counterparty_negotiation_scope","permitted_counterparty_relation","permitted_counterparty_set"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"KG31-L4-SC-L4-b45dc68d0518a2db","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L4","ontological_kind":"SC-L4-b45dc68d0518a2db"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"The scope within which entities can negotiate or trade is limited through communication, partner sets, or contractual relationships; obtaining eligibility does not equal actual pairing or transaction conclusion.","identity_fields":["Permission: negotiation or trading permission limited between pairs of entities","Input: communication relationships, partner sets, or contractual arrangements","Output: the range of potential counterparty relationships; it does not directly specify the transaction pair"]},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::matching.counterparties","paper_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","mechanism_id":"M01","function":"matching.counterparties","source_occurrences":[{"id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R005::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"Any agent is in principle permitted to negotiate with any other agent. Scope: Full peer-to-peer market with the complete communication graph assumed in the study.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Full peer-to-peer market with the complete communication graph assumed in the study.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"a4ec968d194c84c79d11b8c5f4b1ec32c385825f73e90d4fb52db5cab724b54d"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Any agent is in principle permitted to negotiate with any other agent. Scope: Full peer-to-peer market with the complete communication graph assumed in the study.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e04"],"ref":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-matching","page":2,"printed_page":"995","section":"II-A. Peer-to-Peer Trading","anchor_text":"s, nd ng Fig. 1. Pool-based (a) and peer-to-peer (b) market structures. s- s- trading scheme. As suc","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For simplicity, only full peer-to-peer markets are considered here, i.e., with a complete graph as in Fig. 1(b). This graph is for the communication among agents since, in a peer-to-peer market, it is assumed that in principle any agent should be able to negotiate with any other agent. These are market constructs only, regardless of the actual power network (being, e.g., transmission and distribution grids, or possibly a microgrid), similarly to pool-based structures for forward markets, for which actual network constraints and considerations are considered at the balancing stage only. Therefore, when using the term \"neighbor\" in the following, this does not necessarily relate to physical location, but to a direct connection on the market communication graph."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","paper_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","function":"matching.counterparties","source_occurrences":[{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The trading partnership set restricts demand proposals: the bilateral quantity must be zero for a counterparty outside that set. Scope: Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: When a prospective counterparty is outside the requesting agent's trading partnership set. Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration. Participants update requested quantities through their own optimization (6a) and individually send the resulting trade proposals to their trading partners; the negotiation process repeats until convergence. Scope: Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: During each iteration of the negotiation process, until convergence. Requested quantities must be updated consistently with the agent's total-power bounds, with total power equal to the sum of its bilateral proposals. Scope: Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. Condition: At every negotiation iteration. Each prosumer's requested bilateral quantity must remain within that prosumer's power boundaries. Scope: Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). Condition: When the requesting agent is a prosumer. The prescribed update prohibits buying requests from generators by constraining their bilateral proposed quantities to be nonnegative. Scope: Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). Condition: When the updating agent is a generator.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). | Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). | Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. | Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). | Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. | Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.demand","source_rule_sha256":"dd9010023e8d5e8a6fc38f03180aa098dab283f108a8e7f7d06a7719cdb0d88c"},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.supply","source_rule_sha256":"eb3e04d94a468918abeb09de0a73b5857fe82afe93cb43afba9b3825477aa259"},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents. A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price. Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners. The bilateral trade power from agent n to agent m must be zero when m is outside n’s partnership set. Scope: Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n’s trading partnership set. Partnership sets specify possible trades and do not themselves enforce market participation. Scope: Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation. Partnership sets could be dynamically adapted in response to congestion; this is a proposed possibility rather than an asserted implemented procedure. Scope: Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. Condition: Congestion occurs.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. | Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. | Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. | Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. | Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. | Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C006"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"17c2f49b0636db1a4fd2c1e6da9d2ec09881219b8198137ad05850f17c990419"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The trading partnership set restricts demand proposals: the bilateral quantity must be zero for a counterparty outside that set. Scope: Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: When a prospective counterparty is outside the requesting agent's trading partnership set.","expression":"\\forall n,\\ \\forall m:\\ m\\notin\\omega_n\\Rightarrow p_{nm}=0","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","bidding.demand"],"variable_refs":["p_nm"],"parameter_refs":["omega_n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set.","expression":"p_{nm}=0\\quad\\text{if }m\\notin\\omega_n","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E06"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma_{nm}^{\\mathrm{dist}}=\\pm\\frac{u^{\\mathrm{dist}}d_{nm}}{2}, \\forall(n,m)\\in\\Omega\\times\\omega_n, (11); where d_nm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{\\mathrm{dist}} is expressed in €/MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma^{dist}_{nm} = \\pm \\frac{u^{dist} d_{nm}}{2}, \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in \\Omega \\times \\omega_n, (11) where dnm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{dist} is expressed in € /MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E11"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"opa DC power ﬂow approximation of the entire network, is bet- onter suited for meshed networks such ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"While the unique cost allocation policy does not differentiate agents, the electrical distance one might individualize too much grid tariffs. At the image of Danish public transportation system, using a zonal cost allocation policy seems a good compromise between both. In this situation the electrical network would be divided in several zones associated to distinct zonal unit fees. Each zone could be managed by a different system operator. A zone with a high price would incite outside agents not to trade with agents within, and push within agents towards self-consumption. In this sense, the zonal cost allocation policy allows to economically isolate an area. However, its efficiency strongly depends on zones’ design."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g02-E02"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The bilateral trade power from agent n to agent m must be zero when m is outside n’s partnership set. Scope: Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n’s trading partnership set.","expression":"p_{nm}=0,\\quad \\forall n\\in\\Omega,\\ \\forall m\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\omega_n","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R005-x001"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R005-theta001","v141-c-R005-theta002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Partnership sets specify possible trades and do not themselves enforce market participation. Scope: Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e15"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"Note that the deﬁnition of partnership set ωn only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not ","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Note that the definition of partnership set \\omega_n only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not enforce participation. In other words, agent n keeps the possibility to opt-out of the market, i.e. with outcome (p_{nm})_m=0, if its power boundaries allow it."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Partnership sets could be dynamically adapted in response to congestion; this is a proposed possibility rather than an asserted implemented procedure. Scope: Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. Condition: Congestion occurs.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g02-E02"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"p_nm","symbol":"p_{nm}","meaning":"Bilateral trade-power quantity proposed by agent n to trading partner m in the local demand/supply update; the source uses p_nm as the entry of the trade matrix and imposes zero outside the partnership set, role-based sign restrictions, and prosumer bounds.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued bilateral power quantity; p_{nm}=0 if m not in \\omega_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 if n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 if n\\in\\Omega_c; and \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n if n\\in\\Omega_p","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::x::p_nm"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-p-nm","symbol":"p_{nm}","meaning":"Bilateral power quantity from agent n to agent m in the local offered-quantity update; its sign is restricted by agent role in Eq. (6a), and it is zero for m outside n's partnership set in Eq. (2).","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued bilateral power quantity over m in omega_n, with p_{nm}=0 for m not in omega_n (Eq. 2)","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R005-x001","symbol":"p_{nm}","meaning":"Bilateral real-power trade from agent n to agent m.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued bilateral trade power indexed by ordered pairs (n,m) in Ω×Ω","unit":null,"indices":["n∈Ω","m∈Ω"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::x::v141-c-R005-x001"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"omega_n","symbol":"\\omega_n","meaning":"Set of trading partners of agent n; p_nm is necessarily zero when agent m is not in this set.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::theta::omega_n"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n","symbol":"\\omega_n","meaning":"Set of partners of agent n, i.e. agent n's trading partnership set.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of partners of agent n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta001","symbol":"Ω","meaning":"Peer-to-peer market community of N_Ω agents.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::theta::v141-c-R005-theta001"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta002","symbol":"ω_n","meaning":"Trading partnership set of agent n: the set of partners with whom agent n may trade.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["n∈Ω"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::theta::v141-c-R005-theta002"}]},{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","paper_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"matching.counterparties","source_occurrences":[{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements may limit which participants can negotiate flexibility transactions with one another. Scope: Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market. The networked arrangement permits the DSO to negotiate with intermediary aggregators and each aggregator to negotiate with certain prosumers. Scope: Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can be incorporated when aggregators are not required. Scope: Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. Condition: Aggregators are not required. The possible extension could include prosumer-to-prosumer transactions, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil previously negotiated flexibility obligations. Scope: Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. Condition: Additional trading closer to real-time is added.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market. | Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. | Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. | Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E06","v141-b-g01-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"15a22ff1163b4cb25653da0c2dad7bf4c2e3745c5ee2ecf614a93cacf865532b"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements may limit which participants can negotiate flexibility transactions with one another. Scope: Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"to obtain ﬂexibility [42]. ng In [43], it is recognized that within existing electricity mar- o- ket","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"This paper introduces a decentralized market design, which allows a DSO to manage local demand constraints by obtaining flexibility from competing aggregators, which must in-turn incentivize prosumers to provide this flexibility. The novel networked market structure accounts for DSO-to-aggregator and aggregator-to-prosumer ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements, which may limit which participants can negotiate transactions with one another."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The networked arrangement permits the DSO to negotiate with intermediary aggregators and each aggregator to negotiate with certain prosumers. Scope: Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E06"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"p gg g q g the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in m the market are generical","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"It is assumed that the DSO is responsible for ensuring the total demand in the distribution network does not exceed a maximum limit. The proposed flexibility market design allows the DSO to incentivize demand flexibility from local prosumers to achieve this. ICT infrastructure and contractual agreements may be needed to facilitate the procurement of flexibility. Therefore, rather than requiring the DSO to negotiate directly with each prosumer, the proposed flexibility market is designed based on a networked structure, with the DSO able to negotiate with intermediary aggregators, and each aggregator in-turn able to negotiate with certain prosumers. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can also be incorporated if aggregators are not required. Throughout the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in the market are generically referred to as agents."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can be incorporated when aggregators are not required. Scope: Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. Condition: Aggregators are not required.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E06"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"p gg g q g the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in m the market are generical","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"It is assumed that the DSO is responsible for ensuring the total demand in the distribution network does not exceed a maximum limit. The proposed flexibility market design allows the DSO to incentivize demand flexibility from local prosumers to achieve this. ICT infrastructure and contractual agreements may be needed to facilitate the procurement of flexibility. Therefore, rather than requiring the DSO to negotiate directly with each prosumer, the proposed flexibility market is designed based on a networked structure, with the DSO able to negotiate with intermediary aggregators, and each aggregator in-turn able to negotiate with certain prosumers. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can also be incorporated if aggregators are not required. Throughout the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in the market are generically referred to as agents."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The possible extension could include prosumer-to-prosumer transactions, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil previously negotiated flexibility obligations. Scope: Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. Condition: Additional trading closer to real-time is added.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E11"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Only day-ahead trading is considered, but additional trading closer to real-time could be added, allowing agents to renegotiate based on updated demand predictions. In this case, prosumer-to-prosumer transactions could be included, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil their previously negotiated flexibility obligations."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::matching.counterparties","stage":"L4","subtype":"counterparty_negotiation_scope","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"In the complete market communication graph adopted, each participating entity can in principle negotiate with any other entity; neighbors represent market communication connections, not physical adjacency.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: potential negotiation relationships among market participants","Core relationship: permits an entity to establish direct negotiations with other entities","Output: the range of negotiable transaction pairs"],"variation_fields":["The study adopts a full peer-to-peer market with a complete communication graph","The communication structure of the forward market is distinguished from the actual power grid structure","Actual network constraints are considered in the balancing stage"],"constraint_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"paper_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","mechanism_id":"M01","function":"matching.counterparties","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","stage":"L4","subtype":"permitted_counterparty_set","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The partner set ω_n limits the bilateral trading counterparties that entity n can choose: when m∉ω_n, p_nm=0; eligibility within the set does not force a transaction. Network charges may influence the selection tendency among these potential trading partners.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: the relationship between an entity and potential bilateral trading partners","Core relationship: limits the counterparties permitted for trading through the partner set","Output predicate: nonzero trading can occur only if the partner is within the permitted set, but actual transaction is not required"],"variation_fields":["When power bounds permit, an entity may exit with all bilateral transaction quantities equal to zero","Electrical distance charges are intended to encourage trading with neighboring partners; high zonal rates are intended to discourage transactions into that zone","During a contingency, new unit rates may induce a shift to partners unaffected by the fault, retaining the original negotiation process","Dynamically modifying the partner set during congestion is only a possible scheme discussed"],"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C006"],"paper_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","function":"matching.counterparties","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","stage":"L4","subtype":"permitted_counterparty_relation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements limit which pairs of entities can negotiate with each other; the basic structure permits negotiations between the DSO and aggregators, and between each aggregator and the specific prosumers to which it can connect.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: potential negotiating entity pairs","Relationship: infrastructure and contractual arrangements limit bilateral negotiation permission","Output: the range of counterparties permitted to mutually negotiate flexibility transactions"],"variation_fields":["Basic role structure: DSO-aggregator, aggregator-specific prosumer","When an aggregator is not needed, direct prosumer-DSO trading can be added","If closer-to-real-time trading is added, trading among prosumers can be extended to fulfill existing obligations; this extension is not yet part of the day-ahead arrangement in this paper"],"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004"],"paper_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"matching.counterparties","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::matching.counterparties","retained_variants":["The study adopts a full peer-to-peer market with a complete communication graph, in which each participating entity can in principle negotiate with any other entity.","Neighbors represent market communication connections, not physical adjacency.","The forward-market communication structure is distinguished from the actual power grid structure; actual network constraints are considered in the balancing stage."],"evidence_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::matching.counterparties::projection"]},{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","retained_variants":["When m∉ω_n, p_nm=0; eligibility within the set does not force a transaction.","When power bounds permit, an entity may exit with all bilateral transaction quantities equal to zero.","Electrical distance charges encourage trading with neighboring partners; high zonal rates discourage transactions into that zone.","During a contingency, new unit rates may induce a shift to partners unaffected by the fault while retaining the original negotiation process.","Dynamically modifying the partner set during congestion is only a possible scheme discussed."],"evidence_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties::projection"]},{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","retained_variants":["The basic structure permits DSO-aggregator negotiations and negotiations between an aggregator and the specific prosumers to which it can connect.","When an aggregator is not needed, direct prosumer-DSO trading can be added.","Closer-to-real-time trading among prosumers can be used to fulfill existing obligations, but this extension is not yet part of the day-ahead arrangement in this paper."],"evidence_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties::projection"]}],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false,"baseline_parent_id":"COMP-486b93e7e3eb4336","baseline_parent_code":"OMC002","secondary_class":"SC-L4-b45dc68d0518a2db","category":{"id":"SC-L4-b45dc68d0518a2db","stage":"L4","label_zh":"Counterparty permission scope","definition_zh":"The scope within which entities can negotiate or trade is defined through communication, partner sets, or contractual arrangements; eligibility itself does not determine actual pairing or transaction.","main_identity_axes":["Permission: negotiation or trading permission between entity pairs","Relationship: an entity is connected to the permitted counterparty set","Output: the range of potential trading relationships"],"inclusion_criteria":["Specifies which entity pairs can negotiate or conduct nonzero transactions","The permitted scope is distinguished from actual selection and transaction"],"exclusion_criteria":["Directly determines the transacting counterparty based on bids","Only specifies the priority of candidate counterparties","A composite unit with contract selection or stable outcomes as parallel primary objectives"],"supporting_unit_ids":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::matching.counterparties","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties"],"status":"agent_defined_structurally_validated"},"definition":{"definition_zh":"The scope within which entities can negotiate or trade is limited through communication, partner sets, or contractual relationships; obtaining eligibility does not equal actual pairing or transaction conclusion.","identity_fields":["Permission: negotiation or trading permission limited between pairs of entities","Input: communication relationships, partner sets, or contractual arrangements","Output: the range of potential counterparty relationships; it does not directly specify the transaction pair"],"member_unit_ids":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::matching.counterparties","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties"]}},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/87"});
